Wake the ICE session when a signalled peer candidate updates one we
already knew (fixes ICE never being usable on the controlled agent).

CSteamNetworkingICESession::AddPeerCandidate() returned early when the
candidate address was already present, skipping the state promotion and
SetNextThinkTimeASAP() the "new candidate" path performs.

That early-return path is hit routinely, not just on a duplicate signal.
The controlled agent learns the peer's addresses first as peer-reflexive
candidates, synthesized from the controlling agent's inbound connectivity
checks; the signalled candidates for those same addresses arrive after.
So the session stayed in kICESessionState_Idle, Think_TestPeerConnectivity()
was never reached (Think() only calls it in
kICESessionState_TestingPeerConnectivity), no connectivity check was ever
sent, no pair was ever nominated, and GetSelectedSocket() stayed null.

Downstream of that: CConnectionTransportP2PICE_Valve::BCanSendEndToEndData()
returns false forever, so the ICE transport can neither confirm end-to-end
connectivity nor be selected -- and ThinkSelectTransport() explicitly
refuses to activate a transport whose connectivity is unconfirmed. The
connection stays on whatever fallback transport it has, permanently.
Observed with a gateway-relayed DataChannel as the fallback: both peers
negotiated a working candidate pair at the STUN level, the controlling
side had a usable route with a 1ms ping, and the direct route was still
never used. Whether it reproduced depended on whether signaling or the
first inbound check arrived first, which made it look intermittent.

diff --git a/src/steamnetworkingsockets/clientlib/steamnetworkingsockets_stun.cpp b/src/steamnetworkingsockets/clientlib/steamnetworkingsockets_stun.cpp
--- a/src/steamnetworkingsockets/clientlib/steamnetworkingsockets_stun.cpp
+++ b/src/steamnetworkingsockets/clientlib/steamnetworkingsockets_stun.cpp
@@ -1223,6 +1223,28 @@
         {
             (ICECandidate&)c = candidate;
             c.m_sFoundation = pszFoundation;
+
+            // Fall through to the same wake-up the "new candidate" path
+            // does below, instead of returning here.  We reach this branch
+            // routinely, not just on a duplicate signal: the controlled
+            // agent sees the peer's addresses FIRST as peer-reflexive
+            // candidates, synthesized from the controlling agent's inbound
+            // connectivity checks, and only afterwards receives the same
+            // addresses over signaling.  Returning early then left the
+            // session in Idle -- it never advanced to
+            // TestingPeerConnectivity, so it never ran a single
+            // connectivity check of its own, never nominated a pair, and
+            // GetSelectedSocket() stayed null forever.  The transport
+            // therefore reported BCanSendEndToEndData() == false, could
+            // neither confirm end-to-end connectivity nor be selected, and
+            // the connection stayed on its fallback transport for good.
+            // Whether it happened at all depended on whether signaling or
+            // the first STUN check arrived first, which is why it looked
+            // intermittent.
+            m_bCandidatePairsNeedUpdate = true;
+            if ( m_sessionState == kICESessionState_Idle || m_sessionState == kICESessionState_GatheringCandidates )
+                m_sessionState = kICESessionState_TestingPeerConnectivity;
+            SetNextThinkTimeASAP();
             return;
         }
     }
